7. Newly Discovered Independent Review Findings (G44-46–G44-50)
G44-46 — HIGH — setsid() Process-Group Escape, Inode Exhaustion & Per-Process vs. Aggregate RLIMIT Conflation
Affected Component: Static C launcher --bounded-run & G44-35 resource controls.
Preconditions: Hostile Git repo with 50,000 tiny files on an inode-constrained /tmp, OR a child process invoking setsid() (e.g., gpg-agent --daemon), OR repeated sequential spawns under RLIMIT_CPU.
Failure Scenario: (a) Extracting 50,000 1-byte blobs consumes 50,000 inodes without triggering RLIMIT_FSIZE=32 MiB or byte caps, causing ENOSPC during quarantine/cleanup; (b) gpg-agent --daemon calls setsid(), escaping kill(-child_pgid, SIGKILL); (c) per-process RLIMIT_CPU=300s resets for every spawned git command, allowing aggregate CPU exhaustion.
Security Impact: Availability DoS, orphaned privileged/signing child daemons surviving termination, and cleanup/quarantine failure on inode exhaustion.
Why Existing Design Fails: Checked statvfs free bytes but omitted free inodes (f_favail); claimed "zero surviving grandchildren" via kill(-pgid, SIGKILL) without accounting for setsid(); conflated per-process RLIMIT_* with session-wide budgets.
Required Mitigation: (a) Check statvfs.f_favail pre-run AND enforce explicit MAX_TMP_INODES (10,000 max materialized files; batch or stream blobs into capped batches rather than materializing 50,000 individual files simultaneously if inode cap exceeded); (b) enforce session-wide monotonic wall-clock & cumulative child-CPU (getrusage(RUSAGE_CHILDREN)) budget in C; (c) explicitly document that setsid() escape prevention requires OS cgroup v2 (cgroup.kill) or PID namespace (RR-16), and stop claiming kill(-pgid, SIGKILL) eliminates setsid() daemons.
Falsification Test: V46-01 (small inode-capped tmpfs with 10,001 1-byte blobs) & V46-02 (child calling setsid() + sleeping under --bounded-run).
Closure Evidence: Corrected G44-35 spec + V46-01/02 execution logs (NOT RUN).
Residual Risk: RR-16 (setsid() escape without cgroup v2 / PID namespace).
Dependencies: G44-35, G44-40, G44-42.
