G44-49 — HIGH — --bounded-run Coverage Gap for Category C Utilities & /tmp Re-Invocation Path TOCTOU in G44-39 / G44-41
Affected Component: Static C launcher --bounded-run & Category C executable invocation.
Preconditions: /usr/bin/timeout removed from inventory; Bash invokes find, sort, grep, openssl, sha256sum directly, AND re-invokes /tmp/pdfsun-stage1b2-launch-v0.3.5 --bounded-run by path in /tmp.
Failure Scenario: (a) Unbounded find/sort/grep/openssl lack per-call wall-clock timeout & stdout byte caps; (b) under non-root same-UID execution, re-invoking /tmp/pdfsun-stage1b2-launch-v0.3.5 by path in /tmp risks binary replacement between initial launch and --bounded-run re-invocation; (c) unspecified whether --bounded-run re-hashes and fexecve(tool_fd)s the child binary.
Security Impact: Unbounded CPU/disk hang in Category C tools and avoidable path-TOCTOU window on launcher re-invocation.
Why Existing Design Fails: Removed /usr/bin/timeout while only routing git/gpg/gitleaks through --bounded-run, and specified path-based re-invocation in /tmp.
Required Mitigation: (a) Expand --bounded-run enum to cover ALL producer/heavy Category C utilities (git, gpg, gitleaks, openssl, find, sort, grep, sha256sum) with explicit per-tool byte/time caps; (b) open launcher self-FD (O_RDONLY|O_CLOEXEC=0 on /proc/self/exe at startup, passed as --launcher-fd <fd>) OR require root-owned /usr/local/libexec/pdfsun/ in production; (c) inside --bounded-run, open target tool via openat(O_NOFOLLOW), verify its single pinned SHA-256 on tool_fd, and execute via fexecve(tool_fd, ...) (eliminating child path TOCTOU for all --bounded-run tools!).
Falsification Test: V49-01 (find on deeply nested dir exceeding cap), V49-02 (--bounded-run rejects tampered binary via fexecve(tool_fd)).
Closure Evidence: Corrected G44-39/G44-41 spec + V49-01/02 logs (NOT RUN).
Residual Risk: RR-01 (same-UID), RR-10 (for trivial rm/mkdir/mv not using --bounded-run).
Dependencies: G44-16, G44-21, G44-35, G44-39, G44-41.
G44-50 — MEDIUM — Missing --manifest-fd Sealed memfd & EXPECTED_MANIFEST_SHA256 Binding in C Launcher (G44-36)
Affected Component: Static C launcher memfd handoff & G44-36 canonical manifest parser.
Preconditions: G44-36 Section 5.4 assigns the 4 MiB canonical manifest parser to the C launcher, but Sections 9.1 & 13 specify only 3 sealed memfds (script, config <= 64 KiB, optional cred <= 512 B).
Failure Scenario: Implementation ambiguity leads either to parsing the manifest in Bash (reintroducing Bash NUL-byte blindness R-06) or failing recovery.conf's 64 KiB ceiling.
Security Impact: Manifest NUL/truncation bypass or build-time specification contradiction.
Why Existing Design Fails: Manifest memfd was not added to the launcher interface when G44-36 moved manifest parsing into C.
Required Mitigation: Add explicit 4th sealed memfd (--manifest-fd <fd>, MAX_MANIFEST_BYTES = 4 MiB, F_SEAL_WRITE|SHRINK|GROW|SEAL), opened via openat(O_NOFOLLOW) from MANIFEST_PATH in recovery.conf, verified against EXPECTED_MANIFEST_SHA256 (from recovery.conf), and byte-validated in C (schema, sort order, 10-ID formats, zero NUL/CR/non-ASCII, hardcoded Layer B exclusion) before sealing and passing to Bash.
Falsification Test: V50-01 (manifest hash mismatch, NUL byte in manifest, >4 MiB manifest, post-open manifest replacement).
Closure Evidence: Reconciled C launcher spec + V50-01 log (NOT RUN).
Residual Risk: RR-04 (FD provenance under direct same-UID bypass).
Dependencies: R-06, R-07, G44-32, G44-36.
